            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Shakespeare's Writing Style</strong><br>\nShakespeare's writing style was innovative, characterized by his use of blank verse and iambic pentameter. He had a remarkable ability to coin new words and phrases. His skill in crafting characters with psychological depth and his use of complex plots distinguished his work from that of his contemporaries.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Histories and Comedies</strong><br>\nHis histories, such as \"Henry IV\" and \"Richard III,\" were based on the lives of English kings and added to the genre by emphasizing their human complexity. Comedies like \"A Midsummer Night's Dream\" and \"The Taming of the Shrew\" were popular for their entertaining plots and use of language.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Tragedies</strong><br>\nLater in his career, Shakespeare turned to tragedies, producing some of his most profound and enduring works, including \"Hamlet,\" \"Othello,\" \"King Lear,\" and \"Macbeth.\" These plays explore themes of human ambition, madness, and betrayal with unmatched dramatic depth.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
