          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Problem Plays and Romances</strong><br>\nShakespeare also wrote \"problem plays,\" which blended elements of comedy and tragedy, and late romances, which often featured themes of redemption and magic. \"The Merchant of Venice\" and \"The Tempest\" are notable examples that defy easy categorization and are often subjects of academic debate.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Shakespeare's Final Years</strong><br>\nIn his final years, Shakespeare returned to Stratford-upon-Avon and purchased the second-largest house in the town, known as New Place. During this period, he wrote fewer plays, and his last few works were collaborations with other playwrights, indicative of a common practice at the time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Shakespeare</strong><br>\nShakespeare died in 1616 but left behind a legacy that transcends time and culture. His plays are still widely performed, studied, and revered around the world. They have been translated into every major living language and are some of the most enduring works in the English literary canon.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
